+++ b/docs/perf/ghost-network.md
@@ -0,0 +1,173 @@
+# Ghost preview network traffic
+
+Ghost previews show every builder's next block, in the cell under that builder's cursor, to
+every player. In a 12-player test, ping climbed to 300-400 ms once a few people were building,
+and other players' ghosts jumped around. This doc estimates ghost traffic before and after the
+rework and lists what players will notice.
+
+All numbers below are **estimates**, not measurements. They multiply message counts by per-call
+byte costs measured in Studio (remote header 9 B, number 9 B, `Vector3` 13 B, one replicated
+`CFrame` change on an anchored part 25 B). Nothing has been measured in this place yet. See
+[Verifying](#verifying) for how to check the estimate.
+
+## Before
+
+`GhostPreview.client` fired the reliable `MoveGhost` remote from `RenderStepped`, every frame,
+whether or not the player was building. `GhostPreview.server` then wrote `CFrame`, `Color`,
+`Material`, and `Transparency` on a replicated part in `Workspace.Ghosts`.
+
+### Client to server
+
+| Payload | Bytes per call |
+|---|---|
+| `("Brick", Vector3)` while building | 9 header + about 7 for a 5-letter string + 13 = **about 29** |
+| `(nil, nil)` while placing is off | about 11 |
+
+| Frame rate | Calls/s per player | Upload per player |
+|---|---|---|
+| 60 FPS | 60 | 60 x 29 = **1.7 KB/s** |
+| 144 FPS | 144 | **4.2 KB/s** |
+| 240 FPS | 240 | **7.0 KB/s** |
+
+For 12 players averaging 120 FPS, the server takes in **1,440 reliable calls/s (about 42 KB/s)**,
+even when nobody's cursor moves. Each call runs a Luau handler and four property writes.
+
+### Server to each client
+
+The server writes the same values again while the cursor stays in one cell, and those writes do
+not replicate. Several writes to one part in a frame also replicate as one. So downstream cost is
+set by how often each builder's **cursor cell** changes (call it C per second), capped at the
+server frame rate (60/s):
+
+- Per builder: min(C, 60) x 25 B.
+- 12 builders, C = 15/s (assumed for a cursor sweeping across 4-stud cells):
+  12 x 15 x 25 = **4.5 KB/s per client**, and 54 KB/s sent by the server.
+- 12 builders, C at 60/s or more (fast sweeps on a high-FPS client):
+  12 x 60 x 25 = **18 KB/s per client**, and 216 KB/s sent by the server.
+
+### Why ping climbed
+
+KB/s is not the main problem here; the number of messages and how they travel are:
+
+- Reliable remotes and replicated property changes share one ordered stream per connection. Each
+  builder pushed 60-240 reliable messages a second into that stream. A lost packet stalls
+  everything behind it, `PlaceBlock` included, until it is resent. That is the rise in data ping.
+- Client-to-server remotes are throttled at about 500 calls/s per client. A 240 FPS client used
+  half of that budget on ghosts alone, and an uncapped one could exceed it, which delays every
+  other remote that client fires.
+- Ghost moves reached other players as reliable property changes, with nothing to smooth them.
+  When the queue backed up, moves arrived in bursts, so ghosts stood still and then jumped.
+
+## After
+
+- **Each client draws its own ghost** every frame from its own raycast. It no longer waits for
+  the server.
+- **A client sends its ghost state only when it changes.** That means a new cell, a new block
+  type, or showing or hiding the ghost. It sends at most once every 50 ms (20/s), on an
+  `UnreliableRemoteEvent` (`GhostUpdate`). An unchanged state is sent again 0.3 s after its last
+  change, then every 2 s. A dropped or reordered message therefore heals without sequence
+  numbers. A player who is not moving sends one small message every 2 s.
+- **The server keeps state, not parts.** Every 50 ms it sends one `GhostUpdate` batch to all
+  clients. The batch holds only the ghosts that changed since the last batch, plus any whose
+  0.3 s or 2 s repeat is due.
+  Batches are capped at 24 ghosts, which keeps them under the 1,000 B unreliable limit.
+- **Clients build other players' ghosts locally.** Each one glides toward its latest cell.
+- **Late joiners** fire the reliable `GhostSnapshot` once and get every visible ghost back.
+
+### Client to server
+
+Payload `(typeIndex, cell)`: 9 + 9 + 13 = **31 B**.
+
+| Situation | Calls/s per player | Upload per player |
+|---|---|---|
+| Not building, or cursor still | 0.5 (one repeat every 2 s) | 0.5 x 31 = **0.016 KB/s** |
+| Aiming, C = 15/s | 15 | 15 x 31 = **0.47 KB/s** |
+| Fast sweeps, any frame rate | 20 (cap) | 20 x 31 = **0.62 KB/s** |
+
+For 12 players all aiming, the server takes in **180-240 unreliable calls/s (5.6-7.4 KB/s)**. That
+is 6-8x fewer calls than before, all off the reliable stream. When nobody is moving, it is 6
+calls/s (12 players x 0.5).
+
+### Server to each client
+
+Each batch entry is `userId, typeIndex, cell`: 9 + 9 + 13 = **31 B** (or about 20 B for a hidden
+ghost). Each batch call adds about 12 B. A ghost appears at most once per 50 ms batch, so it
+contributes at most 20 entries a second. Every batch goes to all clients, so each client also
+gets its own ghost back and ignores it. That accounts for 1/12 of the bytes.
+
+- 12 builders, C = 15/s: 12 x 15 x 31 + 20 x 12 = **5.8 KB/s per client**, and 70 KB/s sent by
+  the server.
+- 12 builders, C at 20/s or more: 12 x 20 x 31 + 20 x 12 = **7.7 KB/s per client**, and 92 KB/s
+  sent by the server.
+- Nobody moving: each ghost is repeated every 2 s, so 12 x 0.5 x 31 B = 186 B/s. With up to 6
+  batch calls a second at about 12 B each, that is **about 0.26 KB/s per client**.
+
+### Summary (12 players, all building)
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Upload per player, 240 FPS | 7.0 KB/s, 240 reliable calls/s | at most 0.62 KB/s, 20 unreliable calls/s |
+| Upload per player, idle | 0.7-2.6 KB/s, every frame | 0.016 KB/s |
+| Calls into the server | about 1,440/s (at 120 FPS), always | 180-240/s while aiming, 6/s idle |
+| Download per client, idle | 0 | about 0.26 KB/s |
+| Download per client, C = 15/s | 4.5 KB/s, reliable | 5.8 KB/s, unreliable |
+| Download per client, fast sweeps | up to 18 KB/s, reliable | at most 7.7 KB/s, unreliable |
+| Ghost updates on the reliable stream | all of them | none (one snapshot at join) |
+| Replicated ghost parts | 12 | 0 |
+
+At moderate cursor speed, download per client stays about the same. Each batch entry carries the
+user ID and block type, which costs a little more than a bare `CFrame` change. The fix is not in
+download bytes. It removes the upstream flood, caps every rate no matter the frame rate, and
+takes ghosts off the reliable stream so they cannot delay block placement or anything else.
+
+### Scaling past 12 players
+
+Download per client grows with the number of builders moving at once, up to 20 x 31 B = 0.62 KB/s
+each. With 30 players all building, that is at most about 19 KB/s, still under the 50 KB/s
+per-client budget often used for phones. If a measurement shows it matters, these are the next
+steps, in order:
+
+1. **Interest by distance:** send far-away ghosts less often, or not at all. The biggest cut,
+   in a large map.
+2. **Pack batches into a buffer:** user ID as f64, type as u8, cell as three i16 grid
+   coordinates. That is 15 B per entry instead of 31, roughly halving download. It costs a codec
+   and its validation, so it waits for a measurement.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Your own ghost responds faster.** It used to make a round trip to the server, so it trailed
+  your cursor by your ping. Now it moves in the same frame as the cursor.
+- **Other players' ghosts glide instead of snapping.** A new cell is reached over about 50-100
+  ms, so ghosts look smooth even though updates arrive 20 times a second.
+- **Other players' ghosts trail their cursors slightly.** The delay is up to about 100 ms of
+  batching (50 ms on the client, 50 ms on the server), plus both players' ping, plus the glide.
+  Before, the delay was ping plus however long the reliable queue had backed up, which was the
+  300-400 ms seen in the test.
+- **Fast sweeps skip cells.** At most 20 positions a second are sent, so a quick sweep shows a
+  smooth glide rather than every cell in between. A jump of more than 24 studs, such as from a
+  far wall to the ground, snaps at once instead of sweeping across the map.
+- **Rarely, a stale ghost for a moment.** If an update is lost, another player's ghost may sit
+  one cell off, or stay visible after they stop building. It usually fixes itself 0.3 s after they
+  stop, and within 2 s if that copy is lost too.
+- **A builder can still show a ghost far away.** As before, the server checks that a ghost's cell
+  is a real position, but not that it is within placement reach (64 studs). A hacked client could
+  park its ghost anywhere, for example in front of another player's camera. That was already true
+  before the change. Adding the reach check would also hide ghosts aimed beyond 64 studs.
+- **`Workspace.Ghosts` is now client-only.** The server no longer creates ghost parts, so
+  server code (or an admin tool) cannot see them there. No current code reads them.
+
+Block placement is unchanged: `BlockPlacer` and `BlockPlacement.server` were not touched.
+
+## Verifying
+
+The numbers above are estimates. To measure them in Studio, use Test > Clients and Servers with
+4 clients on the old and new versions. Run the same workload on both: every client in placing
+mode, sweeping its cursor across the baseplate for 30 s, then 30 s idle. Compare:
+
+- `Stats.DataSendKbps` on each client and `Stats.DataReceiveKbps` on each client.
+- Data ping in each client's Performance Stats bar while everyone builds. It should stay close
+  to network ping.
+- Time from click to placed block, felt on one client while the others sweep.
+
+Scale the 4-client numbers by builder count to check the 12-player rows. Measure one client at
+240 FPS (with the frame-rate cap raised) to confirm upload no longer grows with frame rate.
